[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Prototype Pattern and Deep vs. Shallow Cloning"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to the Prototype Pattern"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Prototype Pattern is a creational design pattern used when the type of objects to create is determined by a prototypical instance, which is cloned to produce new objects. This pattern is particularly useful when object creation is a heavy operation, such as database calls, complex network requests, or heavy computational tasks."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Shallow Cloning vs. Deep Cloning"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"When duplicating objects, developers must choose between shallow cloning and deep cloning. Understanding the memory implications of both is crucial for avoiding unintended side effects in your applications."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Shallow Copy: Copies all field values. If the field is a memory address (reference type), it copies the reference, meaning the original and the clone share the same nested objects."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Deep Copy: Copies all fields and recursively clones all nested reference objects, ensuring that the original object and its clone are completely independent in memory."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation in C#"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is an implementation of both shallow and deep cloning using C#. Notice how the deep copy explicitly instantiates and populates nested reference properties."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"public class Address
{
    public string City { get; set; }
    public string Street { get; set; }
}

public class Person
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public Address Address { get; set; }

    public Person ShallowCopy()
    {
        return (Person)this.MemberwiseClone();
    }

    public Person DeepCopy()
    {
        Person clone = (Person)this.MemberwiseClone();
        clone.Address = new Address 
        {
            City = this.Address.City,
            Street = this.Address.Street
        };
        return clone;
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary and Best Practices"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Use shallow cloning for simple data structures containing only value types (like integers, booleans, and strings). Use deep cloning when your objects contain complex nested reference types to prevent unintended mutation of shared state across your application."}]
